WEBVTT

00:00:00.641 --> 00:00:04.607
We present ReMeDi. Single-image PBR material estimation, via

00:00:04.607 --> 00:00:08.340
reparameterization between metal and dielectric hypotheses.

00:00:09.786 --> 00:00:11.979
PBR materials describe the reflectance

00:00:11.979 --> 00:00:14.373
properties of a surface for rendering.

00:00:14.564 --> 00:00:16.814
In the metallic-roughness workflow, they consist

00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:20.173
of a base color, a metalness, and a roughness map.

00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:22.751
The meaning of base color depends on metalness.

00:00:22.751 --> 00:00:24.478
Where metalness is zero,

00:00:24.478 --> 00:00:26.966
it is the diffuse albedo of a dielectric;

00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:30.053
where it is one, the specular albedo of a metal.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:33.417
So whoever makes a base color,

00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.777
an artist or a network, has a material in mind.

00:00:36.968 --> 00:00:39.767
We call its metalness the intended metalness.

00:00:39.958 --> 00:00:42.173
A base color intended for metal looks right

00:00:42.173 --> 00:00:45.757
rendered as metal, but too bright as a dielectric.

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:48.868
One intended for a dielectric looks right as a dielectric,

00:00:48.868 --> 00:00:50.717
but too dark as a metal.

00:00:50.908 --> 00:00:54.327
Rendering is plausible only when the metalness matches it.

00:00:55.774 --> 00:00:59.354
Existing methods predict base color and metalness directly,

00:00:59.354 --> 00:01:01.693
with nothing ensuring they match.

00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:05.271
Here, SuperMat's base color on the armor is dark, implying

00:01:05.271 --> 00:01:08.763
a low intended metalness, while its metalness is high.

00:01:08.966 --> 00:01:11.273
So the armor renders far too dark.

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:16.162
In contrast, ReMeDi's base color and metalness match,

00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:18.457
so the armor keeps its metallic look.

00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:23.807
ReMeDi enforces this match by design, with two components.

00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:28.014
First, we estimate the base color twice:

00:01:28.014 --> 00:01:29.855
under the all-dielectric hypothesis,

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.290
and under the all-metal hypothesis.

00:01:32.481 --> 00:01:35.640
Metalness then blends the two at every pixel.

00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:37.183
As metalness rises,

00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:40.450
the blended base color moves toward the metal variant.

00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:43.834
So, as long as each variant follows its hypothesis,

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:47.330
the intended metalness of base color equals metalness.

00:01:48.786 --> 00:01:51.503
But how does each variant learn its hypothesis?

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:53.921
Training data gives one base color.

00:01:53.921 --> 00:01:56.762
We use its metal part to supervise the metal variant,

00:01:56.762 --> 00:02:00.253
and its dielectric part to supervise the dielectric variant.

00:02:00.456 --> 00:02:02.623
The rest is left unsupervised.

00:02:04.142 --> 00:02:06.904
Full supervision would need, for each object,

00:02:06.904 --> 00:02:09.131
its materials, plus a second set,

00:02:09.131 --> 00:02:12.633
fully metal instead, that renders exactly the same.

00:02:12.824 --> 00:02:16.142
No such data exists. So a single network, trained under

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:20.083
both hypotheses, makes the two variants nearly identical.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:23.644
Our second component trains a separate network

00:02:23.644 --> 00:02:27.117
for each hypothesis, so each variant follows its own.

00:02:28.574 --> 00:02:32.404
In short: reparameterization reduces the match to a premise,

00:02:32.404 --> 00:02:34.245
separate networks secure the premise,

00:02:34.245 --> 00:02:37.013
and together, the match holds by design.

00:02:40.641 --> 00:02:41.936
On rendered inputs,

00:02:41.936 --> 00:02:44.448
ReMeDi's base color matches its metalness,

00:02:44.448 --> 00:02:47.710
and its relit results stay close to the ground truth.

00:02:57.041 --> 00:03:00.150
Its metalness is clean and follows part boundaries.

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:31.150
On in-the-wild photos, ReMeDi preserves the details

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.020
of the input and the luster of the metal parts.

00:03:47.052 --> 00:03:48.711
The base color by ReMeDi is free

00:03:48.711 --> 00:03:51.380
of metallic highlights in the input image.

00:04:52.264 --> 00:04:54.161
Predicting all maps jointly,

00:04:54.161 --> 00:04:56.889
base color conditioned on metalness, or both variants

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:00.080
with a single network, still lets them mismatch.

00:05:09.252 --> 00:05:12.025
With ReMeDi, the intended metalness of base color

00:05:12.025 --> 00:05:15.060
matches metalness, by design. Thank you.

